    <article class="editorial-body-wrapper">

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>How the 8th Pay Commission Salary Structure Is Derived</h2>
        <p>
          The salary structure of Central Government employees in India is governed by structured periodic revisions that balance public compensation against prevailing macroeconomic conditions, inflationary pressures, and standard of living indices. Over four million active employees serving across the civil services, railways, defence establishments, posts, and paramilitary forces anticipate the recommendations of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission</strong> to restore real wage values eroded over the preceding decade.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under the 7th Pay Commission, the government abolished the legacy system of Grade Pay and Pay Bands, replacing it with an intuitive, horizontal and vertical <strong>Pay Matrix</strong>. An employee’s monthly salary is not arbitrary; it represents a precise mathematical equation governed by base pay matrices, geographic allowances, and statutory social security deductions. When an employee runs an online calculation, the software determines four distinct components:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Revised Basic Pay:</strong> The core foundation derived by applying the approved fitment multiplier to existing base compensation.</li>
          <li><strong>Geographic & Functional Allowances:</strong> House Rent Allowance (HRA), Transport Allowance (TA), and Children Education Allowance (CEA).</li>
          <li><strong>Dearness Relief / Dearness Allowance (DA):</strong> A compensatory inflation buffer pegged directly to movements in the Consumer Price Index for Industrial Workers (CPI-IW).</li>
          <li><strong>Statutory Deductions:</strong> Contributions toward the Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) or National Pension System (NPS), Central Government Employees Group Insurance Scheme (CGEGIS), and Central Government Health Scheme (CGHS).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Understanding the Fitment Factor: Multipliers Explained</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>Fitment Factor</strong> represents the single most important parameter in Pay Commission arithmetic. It is the uniform factor by which an employee’s current basic pay is multiplied to determine their new basic pay in the revised pay matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          A frequent source of confusion among government personnel is the difference between a "gross wage hike" and the "fitment factor". The fitment factor serves two distinct functions: it first absorbs the accrued Dearness Allowance accumulated over the previous ten years, and second, it applies an incremental real wage hike.
        </p>

        <!-- DATA MATRIX TABLE -->
        <div class="table-scroll-container">
          <table class="editorial-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Fitment Hypothesis</th>
                <th>Multiplier</th>
                <th>Level 1 Starting Basic</th>
                <th>Level 6 Entry Basic</th>
                <th>Level 10 (Gazetted)</th>
                <th>Economic Viability Analysis</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
