        <p>
          As demonstrated above, if the Department of Expenditure confirms a <strong>2.28 Fitment Factor</strong>, an employee currently serving at Pay Level 6 with a base salary of ₹35,400 will receive an 8th CPC base pay of exactly <strong>₹80,712</strong>. This revised basic becomes the basis for calculating all subsequent allowances and retirement accruals.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>House Rent Allowance (HRA) Slabs: City Classifications & Rates</h2>
        <p>
          House Rent Allowance compensates employees for the cost of residential accommodation when government quarters are unavailable. Following recommendations by the 7th CPC, HRA rates were tied directly to the Dearness Allowance percentage. When DA surpassed 50%, HRA rates automatically shifted upward to 30%, 20%, and 10% for Class X, Y, and Z cities respectively.
        </p>
        <p>
          With the implementation of the 8th Pay Commission and the resetting of DA to 0%, the baseline HRA slabs are projected to normalize to the primary structural benchmarks:
        </p>
        <div class="city-classification-grid">
          <div class="city-box">
            <h4>Class X Metros (27% to 30%)</h4>
            <p><strong>Covered Locations:</strong> Delhi NCR, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai, Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Ahmedabad, and Pune.</p>
            <p class="rate-indicator">Highest urban residential cost adjustment factor.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="city-box">
            <h4>Class Y Urban Centers (18% to 20%)</h4>
            <p><strong>Covered Locations:</strong> Lucknow, Jaipur, Patna, Bhopal, Chandigarh, Kochi, Visakhapatnam, and Tier-2 state capitals.</p>
            <p class="rate-indicator">Standard tier covering municipal corporation boundaries.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="city-box">
            <h4>Class Z Rural / Mofussil (9% to 10%)</h4>
            <p><strong>Covered Locations:</strong> All small towns, rural districts, tehsil headquarters, and non-classified military/railway stations.</p>
            <p class="rate-indicator">Baseline allowance protecting minimum housing outlay.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Dearness Allowance Merger & 0% Re-Basing Rules</h2>
        <p>
          A common inquiry among civil servants using our salary calculator is: <em>"Why does the calculator indicate 0% Dearness Allowance?"</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          Dearness Allowance is designed strictly as a floating compensatory payment to protect purchasing power against retail price escalation. As consumer inflation indices advance, the Ministry of Finance notifies biannual DA hikes (effective January 1 and July 1).
        </p>
        <p>
          When a decennial Pay Commission is instituted:
        </p>
        <ol class="editorial-number-list">
          <li><strong>The Merger:</strong> The accumulated DA percentage (which stood beyond 50%) is absorbed directly into the revised basic pay through the fitment factor multiplier.</li>
          <li><strong>The Reset:</strong> DA is formally reduced to <strong>0%</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>The Compounding Advantage:</strong> Although the percentage drops to zero, the employee experiences no reduction in real earnings because the new basic pay is more than double the prior baseline. When the first post-implementation DA hike of 3% or 4% is declared, it is calculated on the new base (e.g., 3% on ₹80,712 equals ₹2,421, rather than 3% on ₹35,400 which was only ₹1,062).</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Deductions: Unified Pension Scheme (UPS), NPS, and Taxes</h2>
        <p>
          Gross earnings do not equal take-home cash. An accurate salary calculator must account for statutory deductions enforced at source on the monthly pay slip:
        </p>
        <div class="deduction-breakdown-card">
          <h4>1. Unified Pension Scheme (UPS) / National Pension System (NPS)</h4>
          <p>
            For all central government employees entering service after January 1, 2004, retirement security is managed under defined contribution structures. The employee contribution is fixed at exactly <strong>10% of (Basic Pay + Dearness Allowance)</strong>. Because DA resets to 0% at inception, exactly 10% of your new 8th CPC basic pay is routed directly to the pension repository every month.
          </p>
          
          <h4>2. Central Government Employees Group Insurance Scheme (CGEGIS)</h4>
          <p>
            Provides life insurance coverage and a savings component. Deductions range between ₹30 per month for Group C cadres to ₹120 per month for Group A gazetted officers, though revised insurance pools may expand these figures.
          </p>

          <h4>3. Professional Tax & Central Government Health Scheme (CGHS)</h4>
          <p>
            Depending on your state posting, state governments levy a professional tax between ₹150 and ₹208 per month. CGHS subscriptions, which grant comprehensive outpatient and inpatient medical coverage, scale with the pay level of the employee.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

